{
  "analysis": [
    {
      "layout": "P",
      "strengths": "The error codes and error messages stay whole and are easy to scan. The meaning and solution columns have comfortable reading widths, and the seven equal-height data rows make the table easy to follow across. It is the shortest scrolling layout and has the fewest measured word breaks of that class.",
      "faults": "The solution column begins well beyond the initial viewport, so the reader must scroll horizontally after identifying an error. Some words still break internally, and the wide table requires substantial horizontal travel.",
      "spatial_logic": "It gives the two lookup columns their one-line widths, then gives the long solutions more than twice the space allotted to meanings. This prioritizes compact rows and continuous prose."
    },
    {
      "layout": "Q",
      "strengths": "All four columns are initially visible, the codes remain whole, and the solution column is the widest among the fitting layouts. It is also the shortest fitting layout.",
      "faults": "Error identifiers and technical terms split repeatedly; the meaning and solution columns become narrow stacks of text. Rows are extremely tall, making it difficult to retain the relationship between a code and its solution while reading.",
      "spatial_logic": "It reserves just enough for the codes, gives the solution the largest share of the remaining viewport, and compresses meaning most heavily."
    },
    {
      "layout": "R",
      "strengths": "Codes and error messages remain intact, and both prose columns have useful reading widths. It saves a little horizontal travel compared with P.",
      "faults": "The slightly narrower solution column creates two taller rows and an extra line in the tallest cell, making the row rhythm less even than P. The solution still starts outside the initial viewport, and measured word breaks rise slightly.",
      "spatial_logic": "It shifts a small amount of space from solutions to meanings while reducing total width. The extra meaning width does not visibly compensate for the added wrapping in solutions."
    },
    {
      "layout": "S",
      "strengths": "All columns are visible without horizontal scrolling, codes remain whole, and it has the fewest measured internal word breaks among the fitting layouts.",
      "faults": "Identifiers and prose still fragment heavily. Its narrower solution column produces the tallest overall table of the three fitting layouts, with very long rows and substantial empty space beneath short cells.",
      "spatial_logic": "It gives meaning more room than Q by taking space from solutions, trading some reduction in word breaks for greater vertical length."
    },
    {
      "layout": "T",
      "strengths": "Codes and error messages remain whole. It requires the least horizontal travel among the scrolling examples, and its uniform row heights provide a consistent rhythm.",
      "faults": "The narrower solution column makes every data row taller than in P, with more line turns through the most important explanatory text. The solution remains outside the initial viewport, and the wider meaning column leaves more unused space in several rows.",
      "spatial_logic": "It compresses solutions and gives meaning more room, favoring a narrower table and a regular grid over compact solution paragraphs."
    },
    {
      "layout": "U",
      "strengths": "All columns are visible at once, codes remain whole, and its total height falls between Q and S.",
      "faults": "The narrow error-message column splits identifiers more aggressively, weakening a primary lookup key. Meaning and solution text remain heavily fragmented, and the long rows make comparison difficult.",
      "spatial_logic": "It takes five pixels from error messages relative to S and gives them to solutions, seeking a compromise between the two prose columns at the expense of identifier readability."
    }
  ],
  "insights": "The first two columns are lookup keys: keeping the numeric codes and error identifiers intact makes the initial viewport useful even when the rest requires scrolling. The solution column carries the most text, so modest width reductions there can add many lines and large amounts of vertical travel. P shows that a wider scrolling table can improve row compactness and reading continuity, although its solution is initially off-screen. The fitting layouts expose every column but cannot preserve the longest identifiers or technical terms at 360px; their tradeoffs need to be judged separately. Measured word-break counts do not by themselves establish which breaks are most disruptive. Proposed widths are hypotheses and require rendered verification.",
